@brunnerh For my reproduction it doesn't matter whether the condition changes or not. The `input` variable value is updated correctly anyway. You can see that in [this REPL](https://svelte-5-preview.vercel.app/#H4sIAAAAAAAAE0VPy26EMAz8lcjtAaRVubMEqd_R9ADBWaLN2hExfSjKv1eBVr3NjOZhZ3A-YIL-LQNND4QeXmOEC8h3rCR9YBCECyTeN1uV4ZR6juKZktp2wtSNhgwNyW4-SsUSUJSnuMv1j6WVP5VWz0kmwcZNIWF7NTR0_ykajoiq49qAXdHeZ_4yoGZPS39wXHSuVUWdo_nJu6O71KHfgsMuq086H0Kp3tx5V46VeRdhUkw2eHvXuWmVHpVlShzwJfCtqRewOz9oyxj4duKhO7OjIbjAgxfvPC7Qy7ZjeS8_v7c_008BAAA=).

The real difference between our examples is that by printing`{typeof input}` you're reading `input` variable in template. In that case, the warning is valid. In my example, `input` was only referenced in `bind:this`, so the template never needs to be invalidated when `input` changes.

I think the fix would be to stop treating `bind:this={variable}` as _reading_ `variable`'s value, as it's a one-way binding.
